    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "It's very, very significant because it's moving our country backwards over 50 years. I mean, when I was in law school, the idea that they would overturn Roe v. Wade was inconceivable. It's fundamentally about gender equality. I think Roe is justified under the 14th Amendment that women should have as much right over their own bodies as men have over our bodies. And they're basically going back to an era where you did not have equality for women. So it is a major, major blow, beyond the blow to reproductive rights. It's a blow to this idea of equality of the, of the sexes. And I think that's why it's appropriately outraged so many Americans. And the people are just grabbing the severity of what this court is doing, how backwards they're moving America. So we can deal with this legislatively. We need to codify Roe versus Wade in Congress. The House has already passed that. The Senate should pass that. And you know, you know, there are two senators who are not willing to overturn the filibuster to do that.",
